PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority still favors B (now ~16/22) under Sale of Goods Act (Ontario) s.19 r.2: risk passes once seller completes act making specific goods deliverable + gives buyer notice. June 2 MacBook not deliverable (screen needed); June 5 repair + text fixing June 6 pickup date satisfies both, shifting risk pre-flood. Defenders (Colleagues 2,5,7,8,9,12-14,16-18,20,21) argue "almost finished + ready tomorrow" + Luke's affirmative reply is adequate commercial notice; statute imposes no hyper-literal post-completion formula. 

Minority (now 6: Colleagues 1,6[switched from C],10,11,15,19) on C: June 5 text is prospective ("almost finished," "will be ready"), not confirmation goods ARE deliverable, so notice element fails and risk stays with Jake. 

Persistent tension: exact statutory wording of notice ("that the act has been done") vs. functional reading; unresolved whether message meets threshold. A/D/E still rejected; no new evidence on insurance/possession. Statute text + precise June 5 wording remain key for later rounds.}

